":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy ready --seen 20'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_14\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc '/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/.venv/bin/python3 -m pathfinder.ledger --root . --actor emmy ready --seen 20'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"21\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}", "{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_15\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I\u2019m ready at ledger entry #20. The strongest lead is a **one round, prize conserving assignment rule**: rank the agents assigned to each job ahead of visitors, then give that job\u2019s existing prize to its highest ranked successful solver. Under known success and token cost laws, and with job choice or idle as the only actions, a globally optimal assignment is a Nash equilibrium. Q permits action dependent rewards; P supplies the fixed job prize and observable correctness outcome needed for this construction. See [Q.tex](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q4P10/inputs/Q.tex:1508), [P.tex](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q4P10/inputs/P.tex:147), and the [derivation](/Users/v/Code_2026/pathfinder/experiments/2026-09-25-repeat-injection/repeat/threads/Q4P10/emmy/research-note.md).\\n\\nThe unresolved publication question is whether the rule improves **net energy and survival** with private capabilities, costly communication, donations, and repeated deactivation. P\u2019s collision counts cannot answer that question: collisions can be valuable, and its comparison changes activity and energy costs as well as coordination. I found no basis to claim improvement in P\u2019s full setting or novelty over prior work.\"}}", "{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_to